<p>After North Dakota achieved statehood in 1889, Mandan's population grew rapidly. The West End school on Eighth Avenue and the two-story wooden East End school on Wright Avenue—now Collins Avenue—soon reached capacity.</p>

<p>In 1899, the Mandan Board of Education, led by {% person "c-e-v-charles-draper" %}C. E. Draper{% endperson %} and members {% person "theodore-a-cummins" %}T. A. Cummins{% endperson %}, {% person "j-h-mcgillic" %}J. H. McGillic{% endperson %}, {% person "h-h-harmon" %}H. H. Harmon{% endperson %}, and {% person "h-d-stevenson" %}H. D. Stevenson{% endperson %}, asked voters to approve a {% inflation 15000, 1899 %} Central School. The building would house junior and senior high school students and provide a larger grade school for western Mandan. Voters approved the bond issue.</p>

<p>The three-story Italianate brick school had a central tower and cupola. Italianate buildings were generally plain and boxlike, with two or three stories, low-pitched hipped roofs, broad overhanging eaves supported by decorative brackets or corbels, and tall, narrow windows with arched or curved tops. Central School's tower had an unusual octagonal bell roof.</p>

<p>Italianate architecture became popular in Europe during the 1830s and reached the United States later in the 19th century. Derived from 16th-century Italian Renaissance architecture and sometimes called “semi-rustic,” it suited a formal public building on the Dakota frontier. The Dakota Territorial Capitol, built seven years earlier in Bismarck, used the same style. Both buildings were constructed with red pressed brick from the large brickworks at Sims, 45 miles west by rail.</p>

