</figure>

<p>The School District owned an entire city block immediately south of the natural ridge marking the city's northern edge. Additional buildings were added over time. A new high school opened on the western edge of the block in 1911, followed by a gymnasium in 1919.</p>

<div class="article-split">
  <figure>
    <img src="/assets/images/gone-forever/central-school-bell-2015.jpg" alt="Central School's bronze bell on its outdoor plinth in 2015">
  </figure>
  <div>
    <p>Alumni purchased a 1,380-pound solid-bronze bell from the American Bell Foundry Company of Northfield, Michigan. It was installed in Central School's cupola in 1914.</p>

    <p>The bell remained there until 1966, when Central School was demolished after completion of a new senior high school. It was moved to a plinth at the south entrance of the former high school, which then served as the district's junior high school.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="article-split">
  <div>
    <p>When the complex was sold to private investors in 2016, the bell was removed and returned to the School District.</p>

    <p>The bell, the original school's cornerstone, and the plaque from the former plinth are now displayed inside Mandan Middle School at 2019 12th Avenue Northwest.</p>
